// engram/internal/events/v1/events.proto
//
// The outbox/Kafka event envelope (decision D6). One Event is written to the per-shard `outbox` table in the same
// transaction as the state change it describes (transactional outbox, as the LAST statement of the transaction); the
// relay hands it to the `index` and optional `kafka` consumers in `seq` order.
//
// Events are THIN: they carry ids, hashes and small scalar changes, never embeddings or long text. Consumers that need
// full rows (an external index) read them from the shard by id. Content rows are immutable (decision N113), so a row
// read by id never differs from the row the event describes, and a fetch that finds no row (a later expunge already
// ran) is a no-op.
//
// The outbox is not a replay log: a namespace move reconciles by set difference and never reads it, so there is no
// event-to-row mapping, no `replay:` annotation and no event for writes that have no other consumer (decisions N124,
// N128).
//
// Bounded size (decision N80):
